<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Ayyubid Emirate of Hama</h1><p class="st-meta">1178 CE – 1341 CE · capital Hama</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The longest-surviving Ayyubid principality, which lasted under Mamluk overlordship and was ruled by the historian and geographer Abu al-Fida.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1178 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontes-river">Orontes</a> to his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taqi-al-din-umar">Taqi al-Din Umar</a>, whose descendants kept it as a hereditary principality within the Ayyubid family confederation.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama's</a> rulers usually sided with the sultans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> against their cousins in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mansur-muhammad-ii-of-hama">Al-Mansur Muhammad II</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1260 and was allowed to keep his throne as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. The principality was known for its learned princes: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-fida">Abu al-Fida</a>, appointed ruler in 1310 by the Mamluk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a>, wrote a universal history and the geographical work <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taqwim-al-buldan">Taqwim al-Buldan</a>. The great waterwheels of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontes-river">Orontes</a> irrigated its gardens.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-fida">Abu al-Fida's</a> death in 1331, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a> Muhammad was deposed in 1341, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> became an ordinary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> province.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1178 CE – 1341 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.14%;width:3.24%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1178 CE – 1341 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hama</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Taqi al-Din Umar, al-Mansur Muhammad II, Abu al-Fida</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ayyubids of Hama</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1260&amp;focus=ayyubid-emirate-of-hama" aria-label="Ayyubid Emirate of Hama on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5611 907 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ayyubid Emirate of Hama lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5931 1163L5811 1337M5931 1163L5815 1335"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5811 1337h0M5815 1335h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5931 1163h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5931 1163h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hama. <a href="/atlas?year=1260&amp;focus=ayyubid-emirate-of-hama">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

